# dnv: the disk node agent of one disk, as a template: one instance for each
# disk, dnv-agent-dn@<name>.service, where <name> is a short name you choose,
# such as 0 or 1 (architecture.md, Components: invocation reference).
#
# Install as /etc/systemd/system/dnv-agent-dn@.service, with the program as
# /usr/local/bin/dnv-agent, the modules of deploy/modules-load.d/dnv-dn.conf,
# the udev rules of deploy/udev/ and, for each instance, its settings as
# /etc/dnv/dnv-agent-dn-<name>.yaml, made from deploy/dnv/dnv-agent-dn-0.yaml.
# Each instance needs its own disk, gRPC address, nvmet port id and NVMe/TCP
# port, which its settings name, and its own local store, the folder
# /var/lib/dnv-dn-<name>, which this unit names and makes: two disk node
# agents must never share a store, and the folder lies outside the default
# store /var/lib/dnv, which a controller node agent may use. An agent that ran
# before with a store in another folder, such as /var/lib/dnv, finds this
# one empty and starts as after a lost store (dnagent.md DN2 and Known
# limits): before its first start under this unit, with the agent stopped,
# move its files, those whose names start with dn-, side- or migr-bm-, into
# /var/lib/dnv-dn-<name>.
#
# The instances of one machine share its kernel, so give them one location:
# create each disk node with no --location, which takes the machine's host
# name, or with one location for all of the machine's disk nodes. Two disk
# node agents of one kernel must never hold the two sides of one leg, and
# only a shared location keeps a migration from putting a leg's second side
# on a sibling instance while another location has room (architecture.md,
# Disk node). It also keeps the legs of a new group on different machines,
# and the new leg a repair allocates off the machines of the group's legs
# while another location has room. A one-machine test cluster has neither
# guard, whatever its locations (for a RAID1 pool it gives each disk node a
# location of its own: the operations doc's "Choose a node's location",
# step 4): every migration there puts a leg's second side on a sibling
# instance, so make no migration on it.
#
# The kit enables nothing. "systemctl start dnv-agent-dn@<name>" starts one
# instance; "systemctl enable dnv-agent-dn@<name>" also starts it at boot,
# once the modules are loaded, configfs is mounted and the network is up. A
# disk node holds no primary, so its start at boot delays no failover. The
# cost of leaving it off: after a reboot the instance stays stopped until you
# start it. In a pool with RAID1, once its sides have been unhealthy for the
# pool's side_unhealthy, 600 seconds at the default, the worker repairs each
# leg they hold onto another disk node, a copy of the whole leg, where the
# leg's group keeps another healthy leg (dnv-worker.md AR8). In a pool
# without RAID nothing replaces the leg, and the IO of each group with a leg
# on the instance's disk fails while the instance is off.
#
# Order: every worker stops before any agent does, and the workers start
# last, once the agents have started again and their startup passes have
# ended (architecture.md, Upgrade in lockstep; the operations doc's "Upgrade
# dnv"). On a machine that also runs dnv-worker.service,
# "Before=dnv-worker.service" makes systemd stop the worker first when the
# two stop together, as at shutdown. When they start together, as at boot,
# it starts the worker's process after the agent's, not after the agent's
# startup pass: a new worker drives nothing for its first --vote-grace-time
# seconds (dnv-worker.md VW7), and it holds its verdicts on an agent that
# answers that it is starting for up to the start hold (dnv-worker.md HL8).
# Across machines, at an upgrade, and for a unit started or stopped alone,
# you keep the order yourself.
#
# Stop: systemd sends SIGTERM to the agent's main process alone
# (KillMode=mixed), which ends its background work as part of its own stop
# and waits for the commands it runs, save one a fence timer had already
# started (dnagent.md SH27), and SIGKILL to what of the unit is left once it
# has exited. Send it no second signal: a second one ends the agent at once,
# without that stop. Restart=on-failure starts again an agent that exited
# with an error, such as one whose gRPC address this machine does not hold
# yet.
#
# Log: the journal. Keep the journal on another filesystem than the store,
# or cap its size (SystemMaxUse= in journald.conf): an agent that cannot
# write its store refuses the worker's syncups (the operations doc's "The
# logs").
[Unit]
Description=dnv disk node agent %i
Wants=network-online.target sys-kernel-config.mount
After=network-online.target systemd-modules-load.service sys-kernel-config.mount
Before=dnv-worker.service

[Service]
Type=exec
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/dnv-agent dn --config /etc/dnv/dnv-agent-dn-%i.yaml --local-store /var/lib/dnv-dn-%i
StateDirectory=dnv-dn-%i
KillMode=mixed
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5s

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
